{"timestamp":"2026-04-23T23:45:33.916Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"1. High, correctness: [cmd/entire/cli/objectsigner.go](/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/1password-commit-signing/cmd/entire/cli/objectsigner.go:34) computes `hasCustomSSHProgram` as an OR across `system`, `global`, and `local` raw configs before precedence is applied, so a lower-precedence custom `gpg.ssh.program` still disables signing even when a higher-precedence scope explicitly resets it to `ssh-keygen`. Why it matters: repos that override a global/system 1Password-style signer back to the default `ssh-keygen` will now skip native SSH signing incorrectly. Evidence: the callback merges config with normal precedence at [objectsigner.go:39](/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/1password-commit-signing/cmd/entire/cli/objectsigner.go:39), but the skip check at [objectsigner.go:51](/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/1password-commit-signing/cmd/entire/cli/objectsigner.go:51) uses the pre-merge OR result instead of the effective program. The new tests only cover “custom in one scope”, “default in both scopes”, and “none configured” at [objectsigner_test.go:93](/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/1password-commit-signing/cmd/entire/cli/objectsigner_test.go:93); there is no case for `system/global=custom` plus `local=ssh-keygen`. Concrete fix: resolve the effective `gpg.ssh.program` in precedence order (`local`, then `global`, then `system`) and only skip when that effective value is non-empty and not `ssh-keygen`; add a regression test for a higher-precedence `ssh-keygen` override.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
